content:
Consider the following three sets of numbers:

Set X: {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}
Set Y: {2, 2, 6, 10, 10}
Set Z: {4, 4, 6, 8, 8}

Which of the following correctly orders the three sets from least standard deviation to greatest standard deviation?

Options: A) Set X, Set Y, Set Z | B) Set Z, Set X, Set Y | C) Set Z, Set Y, Set X | D) Set Y, Set X, Set Z

Consider the following three sets of numbers:

Set X: {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}
Set Y: {2, 2, 6, 10, 10}
Set Z: {4, 4, 6, 8, 8}

Which of the following correctly orders the three sets from least standard deviation to greatest standard deviation?

Options: A) Set X, Set Y, Set Z | B) Set Z, Set X, Set Y | C) Set Z, Set Y, Set X | D) Set Y, Set X, Set Z

I see you picked C! You are half right: Set Z definitely has the *least* standard deviation because all the numbers are tightly packed right around the middle (6). 

But let's take a closer look at Set X and Set Y to figure out which one has the *greatest* standard deviation. This is actually exactly like the trap we looked at earlier!

Both Set X and Set Y have a mean of 6. Let's look at their "distances from the middle":

Set X: {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}
Set Y: {2, 2, 6, 10, 10}

